          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"\u0926\u093f\u0935 \u0907\u092e\u0928\u093f\u091c\u094d\u0935\u093e\u2019 P\u00e2n. V. 1. 122. \u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093f\u0937\u0941 &c.\u2014as Ch\u00e2rudatta, the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"hero of the M\u1e5bch.; and not in vices.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit at the beginning is difficult to read.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Fig. D\u00eepaka, as the common attribute \u0936\u094b\u092d\u093e is mentioned\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"once with regard to \u2018\u091c\u0928\u2019 which is \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0915\u0943\u0924 ( matter in hand )\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"and with respect to \u092e\u0923\u093f &c. which are \u0905\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0941\u0924. \u2018\u0938\u0915\u0943\u0926\u094d\u0935\u0943\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0941 \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u094d\u092f\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0915\u0943\u0924\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0915\u0943\u0924\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0928\u093e\u092e\u094d ( \u0926\u0940\u092a\u0915\u0902 ) K. P. X.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit characters in the quotation are indistinct.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 45. \u092a\u0930\u093f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0940\u0923\u0903\u2014Reduced in circumstances every way (\u092a\u0930\u093f).\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"completely poverty-stricken. \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u0943\u0924\u093f properly means the palm\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"hollowed, hence by Laksha\u1e47\u00e2 what is contained therein; also\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"a measure equal to two palas. \u0938\u094d\u092a\u0943\u0939\u092f\u0924\u093f\u2014\u0938\u094d\u092a\u0943\u0939\u094d governs the dat. See\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"P\u00e2n. I. 4. 56. \u0938\u0902\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0923\u0903 full of, scil. wealth. opp. to \u092a\u0930\u093f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0940\u0923\u0903. Mark\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"the force of the prepositions \u092a\u0930\u093f and \u0938\u092e\u094d. \u0915\u0932\u092f\u0924\u093f ( a verb capa-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"ble of a very large number of meanings according to context);\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"here, thinks, considers.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The diacritics in Laksha\u1e47\u00e2 are not entirely clear.\"},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"\u0905\u0924\u0903\u2014hence; i. e. because of the fact mentioned above. \u0905\u0928\u0948-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u0915\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0902\u2014The state of having no fixity, uncertainty. \u0928 \u090f\u0915\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u092e\u0928\u0947-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\u0915\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0902 \u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0964 The last two lines are somewhat puzzling and\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"have been differently interpreted. For construction see com.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"The meaning is that since there is variability with regard to\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"the greatness or smallness of the same thing it must be con-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"cluded that it is the state of life of those possessed of wealth\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"that causes the things so to appear.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The divided Sanskrit word and its explanation are somewhat unclear.\"},\"r6\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 46. \u0926\u0941\u0927\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0938\u093f\u2014desiderative of \u0926\u0941\u0939\u094d; although a \u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0915 root\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"it is here used with one object. \u0915\u094d\u0937\u093f\u0924\u093f\u0927\u0947\u0928\u0941\u092e\u094d\u2014a Karm. and the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"Fig. properly is \u0930\u0942\u092a\u0915. \u0924\u0947\u0928\u2014equivalent to \u0924\u0930\u094d\u0939\u093f. \u0905\u0926\u094d\u092f seems to\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"have been used here in a wider sense:\u2014now, at present. This\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"verse gives a very sound advice to princes to secure the pros-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"perity of their states. The last three lines contain an \u0909\u092a\u092e\u093e and\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"the metaphor of the first has not been kept up. \u0915\u094d\u0937\u093f\u0924\u093f\u0966 therefore\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"should be taken as in the Com. Cf. Rag. I. 26. \u0926\u0941\u0926\u094b\u0939 \u0917\u093e\u0902 &c.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null},\"r7\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 47. \u0939\u093f\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0930\u093e\u2014murderous, cruel, bloody. From \u0939\u093f\u0902\u0938\u094d + \u0930 added\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"\u0924\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091b\u0940\u0932\u094d\u092f\u0947 by \u2018\u0928\u092e\u093f\u0915\u092e\u094d\u092a\u093f &c.\u2019 P\u00e2n. III. 2. 147. \u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925\u092a\u0930\u093e\u2013\u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925 \u090f\u0935\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 22 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\",\"u5\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\",\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"45\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r4\",\"r5\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"46\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r6\"]},{\"id\":\"u5\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"47\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r7\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]}],\"relations\":[]}"
        }
